{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>One witness shall not rise against a man.</b> It is juxtaposed to the removal of a boundary, to teach you that if one party claims against another that he moved his boundary, he must bring two witnesses. And this is why Scripture juxtaposed 'one witness shall not rise' — the words of one witness shall not be established, for there is no substance to his words for purposes of punishment — neither corporal punishment nor monetary punishment. <br><b>For any iniquity and for any sin.</b> For example, that he testifies about him that he committed a certain iniquity between him and the Place (i.e., between him and HaShem), whether intentionally or unintentionally. <br><b>In any sin.</b> This is a sin of speech between him and his fellow. <br><b>That he may commit.</b> In money. There is no substance to his words to punish him on the basis of his testimony alone, but only on the basis of two witnesses. <br>Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: 'for any iniquity and for any sin' — he does not rise, but he does rise for an oath: if Reuven claims that Shimon owes him a maneh and Shimon denies it, and this witness testifies that he owes it, they impose a Torah oath upon Shimon on the basis of this one witness." "<b>On the word of two witnesses.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: 'from their mouth' — and not from their writing. And the fact that they extract money on the basis of the signatures of witnesses on a document, even though the Torah said 'from their mouth' — this is rabbinic law, so that one does not lock the door before borrowers."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:49:31.228618700Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}